Yuan \cite{yuan2022local} presented a local method to find the compositional inverses of permutation polynomials.

\begin{lemma}\label{ff-}\cite[Theorem 2.2]{yuan2022local}
	Let $q$ be a prime power and $f(x)$ be a polynomial over $\gf_q.$ Then
	$f(x)$ is a permutation polynomial over $\gf_q$ if and only if there exist nonempty finite
	subsets $S_i$, $i=1, 2, \cdots, t$ of $\gf_q$ and maps $\psi_i(x): \gf_q \rightarrow S_i$, $i=1, 2, \cdots, t$ such that $\psi_i(x)\circ f(x)=\varphi_i(x),$ $i=1, 2, \cdots, t$
	and $x=F(\varphi_1(x), \varphi_2(x), \cdots, \varphi_t(x)),$ where $F(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_t)\in \gf_q[x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_t].$ Moreover, the compositional inverse of $f(x)$ is given by
	$$f^{-1}(x)=F(\psi_1(x), \psi_2(x), \cdots, \psi_t(x)).$$
\end{lemma}

